clamp — Limit x to a range                                             core · tier 0 · since 0.5.0 · 1.0 · closed form

USAGE
    clamp(x, low, high)

DESCRIPTION
    The value of `x` brought into the range from `low` to `high`: `low` where `x` is below it, `high` where
    `x` is above it, and `x` itself in between. It is `min(max(x, low), high)`, so an Int stays an Int when
    all three are Ints, `NA` in any argument gives `NA`, and when `low` is above `high` the result is
    `high`, as R's `pmin(pmax(x, low), high)` gives. Given a column, it applies to every row in one pass.

ARGUMENTS
    x           Float. The value, or a column of values.
    low         Float. The smallest value the result may have.
    high        Float. The largest value the result may have.

VALUE
    `x` within the range, a Float, or an Int when all three are Ints; a column when `x` is one.

MISSING VALUES
    `NA` in any of the three gives `NA`. In a column each row is taken by itself: a row with `NA` stays
    `NA`, no row is dropped and nothing is printed for it.

DETAILS
    Exact: the result is one of its three arguments, and nothing is rounded.

COMING FROM R OR STATA
    Equivalents: R pmin(pmax()); Stata clip().

    Zeff                  R                          Stata
    clamp(x, low, high)   pmin(pmax(x, low), high)   clip(x, low, high)

    R has no single function. Stata's `clip` returns missing when `low` is above `high`, where Zeff and the
    R form return `high`.

RELATED TASKS
    - To cap from one side only, use `min` or `max`.

ACCURACY
    Closed form.

REFERENCES
    The definition is the two comparisons `min(max(x, low), high)`; nothing is estimated.

SEE ALSO
    min, max, between

EXAMPLES
    // Above the range, the upper limit.
    > clamp(5, 0, 3)
    3

    // Below the range, the lower limit; a Float among the three gives a Float.
    > clamp(-1.5, 0, 3)
    0

    // Inside the range, the value itself.
    > clamp(1.5, 0, 3)
    1.5

    // Row by row on the shipped wages: one worker's hours held between 30 and 40.
    > let w = dataset(wages).where(worker == 19).select([worker, year, wage, hours, exper])
    > w.add(held = clamp(hours, 30, 40))
    frame · 5 rows × 6 variables · panel worker × year

          worker   year    wage   hours   exper   held
      1       19   2012   12.64    29.2       0     30
      2       19   2013   25.18    31.4       0   31.4
      3       19   2014   35.49    33.5       1   33.5
      4       19   2015   26.07    58.2       2     40
      5       19   2016      NA      30       3     30
